BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T170000
UID:2609067cuGfwM5oE6m94jlPuDBtQ
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist **Henry Frank** in the Del Mue Galleries this Se
 ptember. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the Internati
 onal Day of Peace Festival Finale on **September 20 from 2 – 6 pm**.\n\n
 **J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition** explores Henry Frank’s trans
 formative journey through nearly three decades of incarceration in the Cal
 ifornia Department of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the e
 xhibition reflects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal
  growth while inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for 
 change in people who are incarcerated.\n\n**ARTISTS STATEMENT** **J-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition** explores my personal journey serving a 29
 -year-to-life sentence in the California Department of Corrections as an I
 ndigenous person\, an artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from 
 February 15\, 1994\, to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I
  lived hour to hour. For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the 
 worst and hope for the best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a s
 tate of mind. While many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were al
 so moments of humanity\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed h
 ow I saw myself and the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incar
 ceration became the most transformative period of my life. I began to beli
 eve that I would not die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importan
 ce of serving others\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gain
 ed a deeper understanding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved int
 o: expect the best and be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone unti
 l they give me a reason not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this ti
 me\, I learned to love and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in
  the present\, to be a true friend\, and to serve others with intention an
 d compassion.\n\nThis exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences
 \, emotional trauma\, friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shape
 d me throughout my incarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope peopl
 e can connect with parts of my journey and recognize that I am not so diff
 erent from them. More importantly\, I want to humanize those who are curre
 ntly incarcerated and raise awareness that people are capable of change. T
 hey can become contributing members of their communities. They have famili
 es\, hobbies\, hopes\, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transforma
 tion.\n\nI have been working toward completing this body of work and plan 
 to have the exhibition finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is partially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Ar
 t for Justice Fund. I am grateful for their support and patience for this 
 exhibition to evolve into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry E
 dward Frank\n\nYurok and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:When I Am Among the Trees - Opening Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T190000
UID:260905RohRHlpBOEeDTVJwdyk5yA
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the opening reception of When I Am Among the Trees
 \, a site-specific installation by artist Natalie Joy Tremblay.\n\nCreated
  using cyanotype textiles made with plant materials gathered from Western 
 Hills Garden\, the installation unfolds throughout the landscape as a seri
 es of large-format works that invite visitors to slow down\, notice light 
 and movement\, and experience the garden in a new way.\n\nEmerging as an e
 xtension of the living collection\, each piece encourages reflection on th
 e beauty of moments that cannot be held\, only experienced.\n\nCelebrate t
 he opening with the artist\, enjoy wine and light refreshments\, and be am
 ong the first to experience this temporary exhibition at golden hour.\n\nV
 isitors will receive a commemorative exhibition postcard and map to guide 
 their exploration of the installation. All works will be for sale at the e
 nd of the three-day installation.\n\nThe reception is free and open to the
  public\; registration is required.\n\nArtist Bio: Natalie Joy Tremblay is
  a Sonoma County–based cyanotype artist whose work explores the relation
 ship between plants\, light\, and place. Working with fresh flowers\, foli
 age\, and sunlight\, she creates one-of-a-kind botanical artworks that cel
 ebrate seasonality\, impermanence\, and the quiet wonder of the natural wo
 rld.\n\nInspired by the rhythms of the landscape\, Natalie's practice embr
 aces the unexpected\, allowing light and chemistry to capture moments that
  can never be repeated. Her work invites viewers to slow down\, reconnect 
 with nature\, and discover beauty in what is here\, now\, and ever-changin
 g. She teaches cyanotype workshops throughout Sonoma County\, including at
  Western Hills Garden\, and her work has been exhibited in local galleries
  and collected by nature lovers across the country..
LOCATION:Western Hills Garden
URL:https://www.westernhillsgarden.com/programs/when-i-am-among-the-trees
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wreckless Strangers at the Paper Mill Creek Saloon
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T203000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T203000
UID:2609052IbH3qYh9EusFPJYhB9yqA
DESCRIPTION:Fresh off a busy summer of performances across the Bay Area an
 d tour stops in **Littleton\, Colorado**\, and **Jackson Hole\, Wyoming**\
 , **Wreckless Strangers** return this fall with a trio of Northern Califor
 nia shows\, highlighted by an appearance at **Hardly Strictly Bluegrass** 
 in **San Francisco's Golden Gate Park**. The band will also perform at the
  historic **Paper Mill Creek Saloon** in **Forest Knolls** on **September 
 18** and **Rancho Nicasio** on **Friday\, November 20\,** while continuing
  to build momentum with national radio airplay and a climb up the Top 50 A
 mericana and AAA Singles charts.\n\nWith ***Dirty Souls***\, Wreckless Str
 angers return to their rock-and-roll roots\, delivering their rawest and m
 ost visceral studio effort to date. The six-song collection channels the a
 rena rock spirit of the '70s and '80s that shaped the band's earliest infl
 uences while bringing a fresh\, modern edge to the sound. The first single
 \, **"Dirty Soul\,"** continues to receive national airplay and is availab
 le on major streaming platforms. The full album was released in **March**.
 \n\n$15 at the door. 21+
LOCATION:Paper Mill Creek Saloon
URL:http://papermillcreeksaloon.com/events/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
