BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bye Bye Birdie
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T213000
UID:260910vamdorOiLUCZI-tRe1zIIw
DESCRIPTION:A joyful\, high-energy musical comedy packed with unforgettabl
 e songs\, humor\, and heart\, "Bye Bye Birdie" is a celebration of youth\,
  fame\, and the delightful chaos that happens when pop culture collides wi
 th small-town life.\n\nFrom the high-energy “The Telephone Hour” to th
 e show-stopping “A Lot of Livin’ to Do\,” the heartfelt “How Lovel
 y to Be a Woman\,” and the iconic “Put On a Happy Face\,” this produ
 ction is filled with classic musical theater moments the whole family can 
 enjoy.\n\nCinnabar Theater's production is directed by Zachary Hasbany\, w
 ith book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, and lyrics by Lee
  Adams\, originally produced by Edward Padula. The show is presented throu
 gh special arrangement with Concord Theatricals.\n\nShow details\n\nCreate
 d by: Book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, lyrics by Lee A
 dams\n\nDirector: Zachary Hasbany\n\nLocation: Sonoma State University –
  Warren Theater\, Ives Hall\, 1801 East Cotati Ave\, Rohnert Park\, CA 949
 28\n\nPerformance dates: September 18 – October 4\, 2026\; Fridays & Sat
 urdays 7:30pm\, Sundays 2:00pm\n\nEstimated run time: 2 hours\, with one 1
 0-minute intermission\n\nRecommended for families / all ages\n\nASL-Interp
 reted Performance\n\nSunday\, September 27 at 2:00 PM\n\nThis performance 
 will feature American Sign Language \\(ASL\\) interpretation. Patrons who 
 would like seating with a clear view of the ASL interpreter can contact th
 e Box Office at \\(707\\) 763-8920 or boxoffice@cinnabartheater.org.\n\nTi
 ckets: https://ci.ovationtix.com/35211
LOCATION:Sonoma State University - Warren Auditorium
URL:https://cinnabartheater.org/show/byebyebirdie/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reception and Art Sale: California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T190000
UID:260909of7qs-LZDku9dTifSCKiLA
DESCRIPTION:September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Friday\, 
 Oct. 2\, from 5-7 p.m.\, the public is invited to the 6th California Nativ
 e Creations Art Show artists' reception and art sale\, which features the 
 Tri-County Pomo Dancers.\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native artis
 ts create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in their ar
 t\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The arti
 sts showing or selling work include:\n\n·         Meyo Marrufo\, 
 painter\, guest curator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley Art 
 Museum\,\n\n·         Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood Val
 ley Rancheria\, cultural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·     
     Henry Edward Frank\, Yurok/Kashaya Pomo\, who will show paintings a
 nd work with Legos titled Mind Prison\,\n\n·         Digital arti
 st Nicolas \\(Nico\\) Vargas\, Kashia Band of Pomo Indians\, whose art was
  featured at the 55th Annual Stanford Powwow\,\n\n\\* plus Gloria Carreter
 o\; Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaRose\, 
 Kashia Band of Pomo Indians\; Angelica Smith\,Manchester Point Arena and Y
 okayo Rancheria\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morgan\, Kashia
  Band of Pomo Indians\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenbaugh\; Katie
  Williams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, Big Valley Pomo Tribe.
 \n\n“We are not just people of the past\,” says Rose Hammock \\(Pomo\,
  Wailacki\, & Maidu\, Tribal Member Round Valley Indian Tribes\\)\, commun
 ity leader\, native culture teacher and Pomo Project Board Member. “Ther
 e is great importance in highlighting Native Artists in public spaces. For
  so long\, our communities and their many talents have gone unnoticed and 
 unrecognized - today that narrative has shifted. Now there are Native Art 
 Shows\, Fashion Shows\, and Art Showcases highlighting art and basketry."
 \n\nSays digital artist and native dancer Nicholas Vargas\, “To me\, my 
 art is a living archive\, a digital space where I reclaim our narrative an
 d honor the resilience of my people.”\n\nPainter and bead worker Gloria 
 Carretero \\(Pinoleville Pomo Nation Tribe\\) adds\, “I wish to honor my
  ancestors with native-inspired art.”
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Best of San Francisco Stand-Up Comedy 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
UID:260909IuzdGpwur0eew_WDUS5wDw
DESCRIPTION:San Francisco has played a crucial role in developing some of 
 the most notable acts in comedy history. From Robin Williams and Dana Carv
 ey to Ali Wong and Moshe Kasher\, San Francisco has shaped stand-up comedy
 ’s impact on American culture since the days of Lenny Bruce. Now\, a new
  generation of comedic talent is set to take the stage at the Raven on Oct
 ober 2nd\, so set your expectations extremely high.\n\n**October 2nd\, 7:3
 0PM**\n\n**Gold (Front 6 rows):** $39 **General:** $29 (Includes fees.)
